<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<!-- Metadata about the page itself for search engines -->
<head>
	<!-- shared common Head Logic -->
	<%- include(PageData.PathToRoot+'Components/Head', {
		PageData: PageData,
		ProjectData: ProjectData
	}); %>

	<!-- extra links if needed -->
	<link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="<%=PageData.PathToRoot%>Public/css/Optional/CardHolder.css">
	<link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="<%=PageData.PathToRoot%>Public/css/Optional/ZipperCard.css">
</head>


<body class="dark">

	<!-- Shared common loader -->
	<%- include(PageData.PathToRoot+'Components/Loader'); %>
	<div class="content background">

		<!-- shared common header -->
		<%- include(PageData.PathToRoot+'Components/AccessibilityHelper'); %>

		<!-- shared common header -->
		<%- include(PageData.PathToRoot+'Components/Header'); %>

		<div class="centeredFrame">
			<h1 class="reveal fromBottom center"><%=ProjectData.Title %></h1>
			<p class="reveal fromBottom center">
				A Go web app that works out the cheapest way to eat well. It scrapes UK supermarkets, makes sense of the messy product data,
				then plans my shopping list and recipes around what I already have at home.
			</p>

			<%- include(PageData.PathToRoot+'Components/QuickInfo', {QuickInfoData: ProjectData}); %>

			<h2 class="center reveal fromBottom">Why I Built It</h2>
			<p class="center reveal fromBottom">
				Comparing food prices by hand is slow, because every shop lists things differently: "6 x 330ml", "1.5kg", "500g (2 x 250g)".
				I wanted a tool that could compare a tin of tuna and a multipack of tuna fairly, ignore what I already own,
				and tell me what to buy and where.
			</p>
			<p class="center reveal fromBottom">
				The repository is private, but I am happy to walk through the code in an interview.
			</p>

			<h2 class="center reveal fromBottom">How It Works</h2>
			<div class="cardHolder">

				<div class="card glass reveal">
					<h3>Store Scraper</h3>
					<p>
						Eight supermarkets are described in JSON config, with CSS selectors for the title, price, quantity and image,
						so adding a shop needs no code change.
						Requests are rate limited to one a second, cached to disk with an expiry, and de-duplicated before saving.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="card glass reveal">
					<h3>Quantity Parsing</h3>
					<p>
						Listing text like "4 x 125g" or "12% fat" is turned into typed quantities using a table of regex unit matchers,
						with unit conversion and ratios between quantities.
						That makes price per kilo, per litre or per item comparable across every shop.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="card glass reveal">
					<h3>Tagging Engine</h3>
					<p>
						Over 400 rules tag products against a hierarchy of 600+ tags.
						Rules can be n-gram matches on stemmed, accent-stripped names, regexes, quantity checks or other tags,
						and are combined with and/or logic.
						Rules that depend on other tags are put in order with a dependency graph, and circular dependencies are rejected.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="card glass reveal">
					<h3>Shopping List</h3>
					<p>
						Each item is a condition plus an amount. The planner subtracts what is already in my inventory,
						filters matching products, works out how many packs are needed,
						and picks the cheapest option per store, limited to the shops in my profile.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="card glass reveal">
					<h3>Recipe Variants</h3>
					<p>
						Recipes list ingredients as conditions rather than fixed products.
						The app builds every permutation of inventory items that fits, including optional ingredients,
						and pages through them without generating them all up front.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="card glass reveal">
					<h3>Maps &amp; Admin Tools</h3>
					<p>
						Nearby stores are found with the OpenRouteService points-of-interest API and shown on a map.
						An admin UI lets me create tags and rules from the browser, with validation that stops duplicate tags
						and unknown references from being saved.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="card glass reveal">
					<h3>Web App</h3>
					<p>
						A Go (chi) server with Jinja-style templates and a JavaScript front end using editable tables for products, inventory and profiles.
						The data layer sits behind a generic storage interface, with JSON and CSV backends and in-memory caching.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="card glass reveal">
					<h3>Quality &amp; Delivery</h3>
					<p>
						Unit tests cover the unit parsing, tagging, NLP and scraper post-processing,
						plus data validation tests on the config and a benchmark of the whole pipeline.
						GitHub Actions checks generated code, runs the tests and publishes a Docker image.
					</p>
				</div>

			</div>

			<h2 class="center reveal fromBottom">Screenshots</h2>
		</div>

		<div class="ZipperCardsHolder glass centeredFrame">
			<div class="ZipperCard shaded glass reveal fromRight">
				<%- include(PageData.PathToRoot+'Components/Picture', {ImgData: {
					Path:"Public/Assets/FoodFinder/products",
					ClassList: "expandable",
					Alt:"Food Finder products page showing scraped supermarket listings as cards with tags, prices and unit prices"
				}}); %>
				<div>
					<h3>Products</h3>
					<p>Scraped listings from every store as cards, each with the tags the rules gave it, the price and a unit price that can be compared across shops.</p>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="ZipperCard shaded glass reveal fromLeft">
				<div>
					<h3>Shopping List</h3>
					<p>The cheapest matching product for each item, grouped by shop, with the number of packs needed after subtracting what is already in my inventory.</p>
				</div>
				<%- include(PageData.PathToRoot+'Components/Picture', {ImgData: {
					Path:"Public/Assets/FoodFinder/shopping-list",
					ClassList: "expandable",
					Alt:"Food Finder shopping list grouped by shop, showing item, pack size, price and the chosen product"
				}}); %>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="centeredFrame">
			<h2 class="center reveal fromBottom">What It Shows</h2>
			<p class="center reveal fromBottom">
				Turning messy real-world data into something reliable, graph and dependency ordering,
				designing a flexible rule system, clean service-based Go architecture with generics and interfaces,
				and taking a full-stack product from scraping through to deployment on my own.
			</p>
		</div>

		<!-- Shared common footer -->
		<%- include(PageData.PathToRoot+'Components/Footer'); %>
	</div>
</body>
</html>
